Multiply *A* by itself to make *D.* Then *D* is a cube. [!just IX.3]

Now, since *A* multiplied by itself makes *D,* and multiplied by *B* makes *C,* therefore *A* is to *B* as *D* is to *C.* [!just VII.17]

And since *D* and *C* are cubes, therefore they are similar solid numbers. Therefore two mean proportional numbers fall between *D* and *C.* And *D* is to *C* as *A* is to *B,* therefore two mean proportional numbers fall between *A* and *B,* too. [!just VIII.19; VIII.8]

And *A* is a cube, therefore *B* is also a cube. [!just VIII.23]

Therefore, *if a cubic number multiplied by any number makes a cubic number, then the multiplied number is also cubic.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is a converse of the previous one. When *ab* = *c,* and *a* is a cube, the previous proposition said that if *b* is a cube, then *c* is also, while this proposition says that if *c* is a cube, then *b* is also.
